::MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.4
Question:
A worker earns $12 per hour for 10 hours. What is gross pay?
{
=A. $120#Correct. Gross pay is hourly wage times hours: 12 x 10 \= 120.
~B. $22#This adds wage and hours instead of multiplying.
~C. $100#This does not use the full hourly wage and hours correctly.
~D. $12#This is only the hourly wage, not total gross pay.
}
::MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.4
Question:
Gross pay is $400 and taxes are 10%. What is the tax deduction?
{
~A. $10#This uses the percent number as dollars instead of multiplying by gross pay.
=B. $40#Correct. 10% is 0.10, and 400 x 0.10 \= 40.
~C. $360#This is net pay after tax, not the tax deduction.
~D. $410#This adds tax instead of finding the deduction.
}
::MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.4
Question:
Gross pay is $600 and deductions are $90. What is net pay?
{
~A. $690#This adds deductions instead of subtracting them.
~B. $90#This is only the deductions, not take-home pay.
=C. $510#Correct. Net pay is gross pay minus deductions: 600 - 90 \= 510.
~D. $600#This ignores the deductions.
}
::MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.4
Question:
Which amount should usually be used when planning a spending budget?
{
~A. gross pay#Gross pay is before deductions, so it overstates available money.
~B. tax deduction only#A tax deduction is subtracted; it is not available to spend.
~C. hourly wage only#Hourly wage alone does not show total take-home money.
=D. net pay#Correct. Net pay is the take-home amount available after deductions.
}
::MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.4
Question:
A paycheck lists gross pay, taxes, and net pay. What does net pay mean?
{
~A. total pay before deductions#That is gross pay.
=B. take-home pay after deductions#Correct. Net pay is what remains after required deductions.
~C. the tax rate#The tax rate is a percent, not the take-home amount.
~D. hours worked#Hours help calculate pay, but they are not net pay.
}
